{"id":"b7d87318-c72d-4088-a708-420836ab165a","object":"chat.completion","created":1785009044,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import json\\n    min_cluster_size = case['min_cluster_size']\\n    clusters_input = case['clusters']\\n    parent = case['parent']\\n    cluster_info = {}\\n    for cl in clusters_input:\\n        cid = cl['cluster_id']\\n        cluster_info[cid] = {\\n            'size': cl['size'],\\n            'birth_lambda': cl['birth_lambda'],\\n            'point_exit_lambdas': cl['point_exit_lambdas'],\\n            'parent': parent.get(cid)\\n        }\\n    retained_ids = set()\\n    for cid, info in cluster_info.items():\\n        if info['size'] >= min_cluster_size:\\n            retained_ids.add(cid)\\n    stability = {}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        info = cluster_info[cid]\\n        birth = info['birth_lambda']\\n        exit_lambdas = info['point_exit_lambdas']\\n        total = sum(max(0, ex - birth) for ex in exit_lambdas)\\n        stability[cid] = total\\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained_ids}\\n    parent_retained = {}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        p = cluster_info[cid]['parent']\\n        if p is not None and p in retained_ids:\\n            parent_retained[cid] = p\\n            children[p].append(cid)\\n        else:\\n            parent_retained[cid] = None\\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained_ids, key=lambda cid: (-stability[cid], cid))\\n    selected = set()\\n    blocked = set()\\n    def ancestors(cid):\\n        anc = []\\n        cur = cid\\n        while parent_retained[cur] is not None:\\n            cur = parent_retained[cur]\\n            anc.append(cur)\\n        return anc\\n    def descendants(cid):\\n        des = []\\n        stack = [cid]\\n        while stack:\\n            node = stack.pop()\\n            for child in children[node]:\\n                des.append(child)\\n                stack.append(child)\\n        return des\\n    for cid in sorted_retained:\\n        if cid in blocked:\\n            continue\\n        des = descendants(cid)\\n        if any(d in selected for d in des):\\n            blocked.add(cid)\\n            continue\\n        selected.add(cid)\\n        for anc in ancestors(cid):\\n            blocked.add(anc)\\n    clusters_output = []\\n    for cid in sorted(retained_ids):\\n        clusters_output.append({\\n            'cluster_id': cid,\\n            'size': cluster_info[cid]['size'],\\n            'stability': stability[cid]\\n        })\\n    selected_output = sorted(selected)\\n    return {\\n        'clusters': clusters_output,\\n        'selected_cluster_ids': selected_output\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the task. The problem is about condensed tree stability for HDBSCAN. Given an input with clusters, each with a cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, and point_exit_lambdas (list of lambdas at which points exited the cluster), and a parent mapping (cluster_id -> parent cluster_id or null for root). Also min_cluster_size. Need to compute condensed tree: drop clusters with size < min_cluster_size. For each retained cluster, compute stability: sum over points max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda). Then rank clusters by decreasing stability, and if ties, by cluster_id (string) ascending? The description says \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID.\" Typically ties broken by cluster ID (ascending?). And then select clusters: choose clusters in that order, but a cluster can be selected only if no already selected descendant blocks it (i.e., if a descendant has been selected, then selecting an ancestor would be blocked because it's already represented by its descendant? Actually typical HDBSCAN flat extraction: you process clusters in decreasing stability, and for each cluster, if it has no selected descendant (i.e., none of its children have been selected), then select it; otherwise, skip it because the descendant already covers the points. Then when you select a cluster, you block its ancestors (i.e., mark them as ineligible because they are now superseded). So algorithm: build a tree from parent mapping. Filter clusters below min_cluster_size. Compute stability for each remaining cluster. Sort clusters by (-stability, cluster_id) (descending stability, ascending cluster_id). Then iterate: for each cluster, check if any of its descendants (in the original hierarchy, but maybe only among retained clusters?) have been selected. If not, select it and mark all its ancestors (in the original hierarchy? up to root) as blocked (i.e., they cannot be selected later because they would be ancestors of a selected cluster). But careful: the tree might have multiple levels. Typically we consider the entire hierarchy (including non-retained clusters? Usually the condensed tree only includes clusters that meet min_cluster_size, but the parent links may point to clusters that were dropped. So we need to decide: do we consider ancestors that are not retained? The selection algorithm typically works on the condensed tree (only retained clusters). So we only consider the subgraph of retained clusters. Build parent pointers only for retained clusters: if a retained cluster's parent is also retained, then it's in the tree; otherwise, that cluster becomes a root. The selection: for each cluster in sorted order, check if any of its descendants (in the retained tree) have been selected. If not, select it and block its ancestors in the retained tree. That's the standard algorithm. The output should include clusters array with cluster_id, size, stability (computed). Also selected_cluster_ids: list of selected cluster IDs in sorted order? The spec says \"sorted selected IDs\" but not clear what order: probably ascending cluster ID? Or the order of selection? Typically the output is sorted by cluster ID (ascending) for selected_cluster_ids. But the output schema just says array of strings, no order specified. The instruction says \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" So I'll sort selected_cluster_ids ascending.\n\nNow implement solve(case). Input case is the payload object containing min_cluster_size, clusters, parent, _case_nonce. We ignore _case_nonce. Validate required fields: min_cluster_size (int), clusters (list of objects with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas), parent (object mapping cluster_id to parent or null). Also each cluster must have those fields. We'll assume they are present as per fixture.\n\nCompute:\n\n1. Build dict of cluster info from clusters list keyed by cluster_id.\n2. Build parent dict.\n3. Determine which clusters are retained: those with size >= min_cluster_size.\n4. For each retained cluster, compute stability: sum(max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) for exit_lambda in point_exit_lambdas). Note: birth_lambda is given per cluster.\n5. Build tree of retained clusters: for each retained cluster, find its parent (from parent dict) if parent is also retained. Then that parent's children list includes this cluster. Also track roots (retained clusters with no retained parent).\n6. Sort retained clusters by (-stability, cluster_id) (string compare).\n7. Initialize selected = set(), blocked = set(). (Blocked are clusters that cannot be selected because an ancestor already selected? Actually \"block its ancestors\" means after selecting a cluster, its ancestors are blocked from selection. So if a cluster is selected, its ancestors become blocked. Also if a cluster has a selected descendant, it is blocked (cannot be selected). So we can maintain a set of selected clusters. Then for each candidate in sorted order: if candidate is in blocked: skip. Else, check if any descendant (in retained tree) is in selected. If yes, skip (blocked). Else, select candidate, add to selected, and add all its ancestors (in retained tree) to blocked. Also note: ancestors can be multiple levels; we need to traverse up from candidate's parent until root.\n8. After processing all, selected_cluster_ids = sorted(list(selected)) (ascending string order).\n9. Build output: clusters array of objects with cluster_id, size, stability for all retained clusters (including those not selected). Use stability values computed. Then selected_cluster_ids.\n\nEdge cases: size < min_cluster_size -> drop them entirely. Also need to ensure that stability is computed only for retained clusters. Note: the point_exit_lambdas list may include values less than birth_lambda? Then max(0, ...) ensures non-negative.\n\nImplementation details: use Python's json module to output. Function returns dict.\n\nNow produce the Python code as a JSON string with escape characters.\n\nWrite solve(case) function. Ensure no imports beyond standard library; use typing if needed but not necessary. We'll not use any external modules.\n\nWe'll enclose the code in a string and then the output JSON object with key \"implementation\". The code must be a valid Python source.\n\nLet's write code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    import json\n    # Validate required keys?\n    min_cluster_size = case['min_cluster_size']\n    clusters_input = case['clusters']\n    parent = case['parent']\n    # Build cluster dict\n    cluster_info = {}\n    for cl in clusters_input:\n        cid = cl['cluster_id']\n        cluster_info[cid] = {\n            'size': cl['size'],\n            'birth_lambda': cl['birth_lambda'],\n            'point_exit_lambdas': cl['point_exit_lambdas'],\n            'parent': parent.get(cid)\n        }\n    # Determine retained\n    retained_ids = set()\n    for cid, info in cluster_info.items():\n        if info['size'] >= min_cluster_size:\n            retained_ids.add(cid)\n    # Compute stability for retained\n    stability = {}\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        info = cluster_info[cid]\n        birth = info['birth_lambda']\n        exit_lambdas = info['point_exit_lambdas']\n        total = sum(max(0, ex - birth) for ex in exit_lambdas)\n        stability[cid] = total\n    # Build tree among retained\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained_ids}\n    parent_retained = {}\n    for cid in retained_ids:\n        p = cluster_info[cid]['parent']\n        if p is not None and p in retained_ids:\n            parent_retained[cid] = p\n            children[p].append(cid)\n        else:\n            parent_retained[cid] = None\n    # Sort retained by (-stability, cid)\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained_ids, key=lambda cid: (-stability[cid], cid))\n    # Selection\n    selected = set()\n    blocked = set()\n    # Helper to get ancestors\n    def ancestors(cid):\n        anc = []\n        cur = cid\n        while parent_retained[cur] is not None:\n            cur = parent_retained[cur]\n            anc.append(cur)\n        return anc\n    # Helper to get all descendants using DFS\n    def descendants(cid):\n        des = []\n        stack = [cid]\n        while stack:\n            node = stack.pop()\n            for child in children[node]:\n                des.append(child)\n                stack.append(child)\n        return des\n    for cid in sorted_retained:\n        if cid in blocked:\n            continue\n        # Check if any descendant is selected\n        des = descendants(cid)\n        has_selected_desc = any(d in selected for d in des)\n        if has_selected_desc:\n            blocked.add(cid)\n            continue\n        # Select this cluster\n        selected.add(cid)\n        # Block its ancestors\n        for anc in ancestors(cid):\n            blocked.add(anc)\n    # Build output clusters list: all retained clusters with cluster_id, size, stability\n    clusters_output = []\n    for cid in sorted(retained_ids):\n        clusters_output.append({\n            'cluster_id': cid,\n            'size': cluster_info[cid]['size'],\n            'stability': stability[cid]\n        })\n    selected_output = sorted(selected)\n    return {\n        'clusters': clusters_output,\n        'selected_cluster_ids': selected_output\n    }\n\nBut careful: The output schema requires clusters to be an array, and we need to include all retained clusters, not sorted by anything. The spec says \"Return all retained stability rows\" and the output schema doesn't specify order. We can sort by cluster_id for deterministic output. That's fine.\n\nNow about tie handling: \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID.\" That's what we did in sorting. When selecting, we use that order.\n\nNow we need to ensure that we treat the tree correctly when a cluster's parent is not retained. In that case it's a root of the retained tree.\n\nNow the output should be a JSON object with those two keys.\n\nNow the code must be enclosed in a JSON string for the \"implementation\" value.\n\nWe'll produce the final answer as:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import json\\n    min_cluster_size = case['min_cluster_size']\\n    clusters_input = case['clusters']\\n    parent = case['parent']\\n    cluster_info = {}\\n    for cl in clusters_input:\\n        cid = cl['cluster_id']\\n        cluster_info[cid] = {\\n            'size': cl['size'],\\n            'birth_lambda': cl['birth_lambda'],\\n            'point_exit_lambdas': cl['point_exit_lambdas'],\\n            'parent': parent.get(cid)\\n        }\\n    retained_ids = set()\\n    for cid, info in cluster_info.items():\\n        if info['size'] >= min_cluster_size:\\n            retained_ids.add(cid)\\n    stability = {}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        info = cluster_info[cid]\\n        birth = info['birth_lambda']\\n        exit_lambdas = info['point_exit_lambdas']\\n        total = sum(max(0, ex - birth) for ex in exit_lambdas)\\n        stability[cid] = total\\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained_ids}\\n    parent_retained = {}\\n    for cid in retained_ids:\\n        p = cluster_info[cid]['parent']\\n        if p is not None and p in retained_ids:\\n            parent_retained[cid] = p\\n            children[p].append(cid)\\n        else:\\n            parent_retained[cid] = None\\n    sorted_retained = sorted(retained_ids, key=lambda cid: (-stability[cid], cid))\\n    selected = set()\\n    blocked = set()\\n    def ancestors(cid):\\n        anc = []\\n        cur = cid\\n        while parent_retained[cur] is not None:\\n            cur = parent_retained[cur]\\n            anc.append(cur)\\n        return anc\\n    def descendants(cid):\\n        des = []\\n        stack = [cid]\\n        while stack:\\n            node = stack.pop()\\n            for child in children[node]:\\n                des.append(child)\\n                stack.append(child)\\n        return des\\n    for cid in sorted_retained:\\n        if cid in blocked:\\n            continue\\n        des = descendants(cid)\\n        if any(d in selected for d in des):\\n            blocked.add(cid)\\n            continue\\n        selected.add(cid)\\n        for anc in ancestors(cid):\\n            blocked.add(anc)\\n    clusters_output = []\\n    for cid in sorted(retained_ids):\\n        clusters_output.append({\\n            'cluster_id': cid,\\n            'size': cluster_info[cid]['size'],\\n            'stability': stability[cid]\\n        })\\n    selected_output = sorted(selected)\\n    return {\\n        'clusters': clusters_output,\\n        'selected_cluster_ids': selected_output\\n    }\"\n}\n\nBut need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes properly. In JSON, newlines in strings are represented as \\n. So we need to replace actual newlines in the code with \\n. Also backslashes must be escaped. In the code we have backslashes inside sum line? No. But we have string literals using single quotes, which is fine. The code itself uses double quotes? We used single quotes throughout. So no conflict. However, the JSON string must escape double quotes inside the string, but we have none. So we can just replace newlines. Also we need to escape backslashes (none). So the implementation string will be a single line with \\n escapes.\n\nLet's write the code as a single line with \\n. I'll produce the final JSON.\n\nBut note: The code as written should be valid Python. I should ensure indentation is preserved using \\n. Use 4 spaces per indent.\n\nI'll write it carefully.\n\nWe'll also add docstring? Not needed.\n\nNow produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1609,"completion_tokens":3761,"total_tokens":5370,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1536},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3074},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1536,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":73},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}